const PREGEN_SIMP = {"0.0.hi": "Socho tum ek waiter ko order de rahe ho — har baar poora order dobara bolna padta hai, waiter pichhli baat yaad nahi rakhta.\n\nClaude API bhi aisa hi hai: har request ek complete package hoti hai jisme **model** (kaunsa Claude), **max_tokens** (kitna lamba jawab), **system** (Claude ko kaise behave karna hai), **messages** (poori conversation) — sab kuch hota hai. Claude ko tools bhi diye ja sakte hain (`tools`), aur `tool_choice` batata hai ki tool use karna zaroori hai ya optional.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `messages` array me **poori conversation history** bhejni padti hai — Claude khud kuch yaad nahi rakhta\n- `system` field alag hoti hai, aur poore conversation me apply hoti hai\n- `tools` aur `tool_choice` decide karte hain Claude kya-kya call kar sakta hai", "0.1.hi": "Ek chat me do log baat karte hain — `user` aur `assistant`. Lekin jab Claude koi tool use karta hai (jaise database check karna), uska result bhi ek `user`-role message ke andar hi bheja jaata hai, ek special `tool_result` block ke saath — koi alag \"tool\" role nahi hoti.\n\n`system` normally top-level field me hoti hai, lekin ise beech conversation me bhi ek special message ki tarah insert kiya ja sakta hai — jaise mid-way naye instructions dena bina purani cached history invalidate kiye. Iske kuch strict placement rules hain (kis ke baad aa sakti hai, kis se pehle nahi).\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Tool result = `user` role ke andar `tool_result` block, koi alag role nahi\n- Har API call independent hai — Claude ko poori history baar-baar bhejni padti hai\n- Mid-conversation `system` message baad wale turns ke liye pehle wale ko override kar deta hai", "0.2.hi": "`stop_reason` Claude ke jawab ka \"reason code\" hai — ye batata hai Claude ne generate karna kyun roka.\n\nSabse important do values: `tool_use` (Claude ek tool chalana chahta hai — tumhe execute karke result wapas dena hai) aur `end_turn` (Claude ka jawab complete hai — user ko dikhao). Baaki: `max_tokens` (limit khatam ho gayi, jawab adhoora ho sakta hai) aur `stop_sequence`.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Agentic loops ka control isi field pe based hota hai\n- `tool_use` = tool chalao aur result wapas bhejo; `end_turn` = kaam khatam\n- Sirf Claude ka text dekh ke ya guess karke completion decide mat karo — `stop_reason` hi sahi signal hai", "0.3.hi": "System prompt ek tarah ka \"job description\" hai jo Claude ko conversation shuru hote hi diya jaata hai — ye batata hai Claude kaun hai, kaise behave karna hai, kya rules follow karne hain.\n\nYe user messages se zyada priority rakhta hai aur poori conversation me constant rehta hai. Lekin sawdhaan: agar system prompt me koi wording jaise \"customer ko hamesha verify karo\" hai, toh Claude usse zyada seriously le sakta hai aur zaroorat na hone par bhi verification tool overuse kar sakta hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- System prompt = role, constraints, output format define karne ki jagah\n- Wording bohot matter karti hai — chhoti si phrase bhi unintended tool-usage patterns bana sakti hai\n- Ye `messages` array ka hissa nahi, alag field hai", "0.4.hi": "Context window Claude ki \"working memory\" hai — jitna text (tokens me) woh ek waqt me dekh sakta hai. Isme system prompt, poori message history, tool definitions, aur tool results — sab shamil hota hai.\n\nTeen common problems: (1) **Lost-in-the-middle** — Claude shuruat aur end ka content acche se yaad rakhta hai, beech ka miss kar sakta hai (jaise ek lambi kitaab ka beech ka chapter bhool jaana). (2) **Tool result accumulation** — agar ek tool 40 fields return kare aur sirf 5 kaam ke hon, baaki 35 bekar me space le rahe hain. (3) **Progressive summarization** — jab history ko summarize karte hain, exact numbers/dates \"kuch\", \"lagbhag\" jaise vague words me badal jaate hain.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Important info ko shuruat ya end me rakho, beech me nahi\n- Tool outputs ko trim karo — sirf zaroori fields rakho\n- Summarization me numbers/dates khaas dhyan se preserve karo, warna precision kho jaati hai", "1.0.hi": "Socho Claude ek manager hai jo khud coding nahi karta, bas team members ko instructions deta hai kya karna hai. `tool_use` bhi waisa hi hai — Claude khud code run nahi karta; woh bolta hai \"ye function chalao, ye parameters ke saath\", aur tumhara code use actually execute karta hai, phir result Claude ko wapas deta hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Claude sirf request generate karta hai, execution tumhara code karta hai\n- Ye Claude ko \"real world\" se connect karne ka main tareeka hai (database, API, files, sab kuch)", "1.1.hi": "Har tool ek JSON schema se define hota hai — ismein naam, description, aur input format hota hai. Sabse important cheez hai **description** — ye Claude ka primary tareeka hai decide karne ka ki kaunsa tool kab use karna hai.\n\nAgar description minimal hai (\"customer info retrieve karta hai\"), Claude confuse ho sakta hai jab do tools similar lagein. Description me hona chahiye: tool kya karta hai, kya return karta hai, input format ke examples, edge cases, aur kab is tool ko doosre similar tool ki jagah use karna hai. Built-in tools (jaise Read, Grep) aur MCP tools ke beech bhi Claude confuse ho sakta hai agar MCP tool ki description weak ho.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Tool description = model ka **primary selection mechanism** — isse detailed banao\n- Do tools ki description kabhi bhi ek jaisi ya overlapping mat rakho\n- Har description me batao: kya karta hai, format kya hai, kab use karna hai vs doosre tool ke against", "1.2.hi": "`tool_choice` control karta hai ki Claude tool select karne me kitni azaadi rakhta hai. Teen options: `auto` (Claude khud decide kare — default), `any` (Claude ko koi na koi tool zaroor call karna hai), aur ek specific tool ka naam (Claude ko exactly wahi tool call karna hoga).\n\nYe kaam aata hai jab tumhe guarantee chahiye ki structured output mile (`any` use karo), ya jab ek specific pehla step force karna ho (jaise pehle `extract_metadata` hi chalna chahiye).\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `auto` = default, flexible\n- `any` = structured output guarantee ke liye\n- Specific tool name = forced first step ke liye", "1.3.hi": "Agar tumhe Claude se structured JSON chahiye (jaise ek form fill karwana), `tool_use` + JSON schema sabse reliable tareeka hai. Ye guarantee karta hai ki JSON syntactically sahi hoga (koi missing bracket nahi) aur required fields honge — lekin ye guarantee nahi karta ki **values sahi honge** (Claude phir bhi galat data de sakta hai).\n\nAcha schema design karne ke rules: (1) fields ko required mat banao agar data hamesha available nahi hota — warna Claude fake values bana dega. (2) Missing info ke liye nullable fields use karo (`null` allow karo) taaki Claude hallucinate na kare. (3) Categories (enums) me ek \"other\" option rakho taaki unexpected cases handle ho sake. (4) Agar Claude confident nahi hai, ek \"unclear\" option achha hai — galat category se better hai honest \"pata nahi\".\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- JSON schema syntax guarantee karta hai, meaning nahi\n- Nullable fields aur \"other\"/\"unclear\" options hallucination rokte hain\n- Sirf zaroori fields ko `required` banao", "1.4.hi": "Do tarah ki galtiyan hoti hain: **Syntax errors** (jaise invalid JSON, galat field type) aur **Semantic errors** (jaise numbers add nahi ho rahe, galat field me value, hallucination).\n\nSyntax errors ko `tool_use` + JSON schema use karke completely eliminate kiya ja sakta hai. Lekin semantic errors alag hain — inke liye validation checks, retry-with-feedback, ya self-correction chahiye hota hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Syntax fix = schema enforcement (100% solvable)\n- Semantic fix = validation + retry logic (schema isse nahi bachaata)", "2.0.hi": "Ek chef ko socho jo recipe follow karta hai step-by-step: ingredient uthao, check karo, agla step karo, jab tak dish ban na jaaye. Agentic loop bhi waisa hi hai — Claude ek hi jawab nahi deta, balki actions ki ek series perform karta hai: request bhejo → response check karo → agar `tool_use` hai toh tool chalao aur result wapas bhejo → phir se check karo → jab `end_turn` mile, ruk jaao.\n\nYe **model-driven** hai — Claude khud decide karta hai agla tool kaunsa chalana hai, based on context. Ye fixed decision-tree se alag hai jahan sequence pehle se hardcoded ho.\n\n**Galtiyan jo mat karo:** Claude ke text me \"Task completed\" jaisa phrase dhoondh ke loop rokna, ya ek arbitrary limit (jaise max 5 iterations) use karna, ya sirf text content dekh ke maan lena kaam ho gaya.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Sirf ek reliable signal hai: `stop_reason == \"end_turn\"`\n- Ye model-driven hai, hardcoded sequence nahi", "2.1.hi": "`AgentDefinition` ek agent ka \"configuration card\" hai — isme hota hai naam, description, system_prompt (instructions), aur `allowed_tools` (kaunse tools use kar sakta hai).\n\n`allowed_tools` khaas important hai — ye \"least privilege\" ka principle follow karta hai: agent ko sirf wahi tools do jo use zaroorat hai, extra nahi.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `allowed_tools` = security aur focus dono ke liye zaroori\n- Coordinator agent ke `allowed_tools` me `\"Task\"` hona chahiye taaki woh subagents spawn kar sake", "2.2.hi": "Hub-and-spoke ek architecture pattern hai jaha ek **coordinator** beech me baitha hota hai aur multiple **subagents** (jaise search, analysis, synthesis) uske around kaam karte hain — bilkul ek manager aur uski team jaisa.\n\nCoordinator ka kaam: task todna, sahi subagents choose karna, kaam delegate karna, results collect karna, errors handle karna, aur final answer user ko dena.\n\n**Sabse zaroori principle:** subagents ke paas **isolated context** hota hai — woh coordinator ki poori conversation history apne aap nahi dekh sakte. Jo bhi context chahiye, use explicitly unke prompt me dena padta hai. Saari communication coordinator ke through hi hoti hai (taaki observability aur control mile).\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Subagents apne aap kuch yaad nahi rakhte — sab kuch explicitly dena padta hai\n- Saari baat-cheet coordinator ke through hoti hai, subagents aapas me directly baat nahi karte", "2.3.hi": "`Task` tool wo mechanism hai jisse coordinator naye subagents spawn karta hai. Ek galat tareeka: subagent ko sirf \"Analyze the document\" bolna (koi context nahi). Sahi tareeka: pura context dena — document text, pichhle search results, expected output format — sab kuch prompt me likh dena.\n\nEk khaas power feature: coordinator apne ek hi response me **multiple Tasks parallel** me spawn kar sakta hai — jaise ek saath 3 alag subagents ko 3 alag kaam sonp dena, sab ek saath chalte hain.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Subagent ko explicit context dena mandatory hai\n- Parallel Task calls = ek response me multiple subagents ek saath kaam kar sakte hain", "2.4.hi": "Hooks ek tarah ke \"checkpoint guards\" hain jo agent lifecycle ke specific points pe intercept karte hain. Do main types: **PostToolUse** (tool ka result Claude tak pahunchne se pehle usse modify/normalize karta hai — jaise date formats ko consistent karna) aur **PreToolUse** (koi action hone se pehle usse block kar sakta hai — jaise $500 se zyada ke refund ko rok dena).\n\nSabse important difference: **Hooks deterministic hote hain (100% guarantee)**, jabki **prompt instructions probabilistic hote hain (>90%, kabhi 100% nahi)**. Rule simple hai: agar failure ka financial, legal, ya safety consequence hai, hooks use karo, sirf prompts pe rely mat karo.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Hooks = code-level guarantee (kabhi break nahi hoga)\n- Prompts = suggestions (Claude follow kare, but guarantee nahi)\n- Critical business rules → hooks; general preferences → prompts", "3.0.hi": "MCP (Model Context Protocol) ek standard \"plug\" hai jisse Claude ko bahar ki systems se connect karte hain — jaise ek USB port jo kisi bhi compatible device ko jod deta hai.\n\nMCP teen cheezein define karta hai: **Tools** (functions jo actions perform karte hain — CRUD, API calls, commands), **Resources** (data jo agent read kar sakta hai context ke liye — docs, schemas), aur **Prompts** (common tasks ke liye ready-made templates).\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Tools = actions, Resources = read-only context data, Prompts = templates\n- Ye ek open standard hai jo har jagah kaam kare", "3.1.hi": "Ek MCP server ek process hai jo MCP protocol implement karta hai aur tools/resources provide karta hai. Jab tum ek MCP server se connect karte ho, uske saare tools automatically discover ho jaate hain, aur agar kai servers connected hain toh unke saare tools ek saath available ho jaate hain.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Ek server connect karo, uske saare tools mil jaate hain\n- Tool descriptions hi decide karti hain model unhe kaise use karega", "3.2.hi": "MCP servers ko configure karne ke do jagah hain: **Project config** (`.mcp.json`) — poori team ke liye, version control me rakha jaata hai, secrets ke liye environment variables use hote hain (jaise `${GITHUB_TOKEN}`) taaki actual token kabhi commit na ho. Aur **User config** (`~/.claude.json`) — personal experiments ke liye, version control me nahi jaata.\n\nStandard integrations (Jira, GitHub, Slack) ke liye existing community MCP servers use karo; apna khud ka server tab banao jab kaam bilkul unique/team-specific ho.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Team ke liye: `.mcp.json` + env variables (secrets safe rehte hain)\n- Personal ke liye: `~/.claude.json`\n- Existing servers pehle try karo, khud banane se pehle", "3.3.hi": "Jab ek MCP tool fail hota hai, woh response me `isError: true` set karta hai — ye agent ko batata hai ki call fail hui.\n\nEk **acha error** structured hota hai — usme batata hai error kaunse type ka hai (jaise \"transient\"), kya retry kiya ja sakta hai, exact message, aur kya partial results mile. Ek **generic error** (bas \"Operation failed\") agent ko kuch bhi decide karne ke liye information nahi deta — retry kare, query change kare, ya escalate kare, use pata hi nahi chalta.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Achi error response = structured, detailed, retry-info ke saath\n- Generic error = agent ke liye bekar, decision-making impossible ho jaati hai", "3.4.hi": "Resources woh data hai jo agent bina koi action liye padh sakta hai — jaise ek content catalog (saari tasks ki list), database schema, ya documentation.\n\nInka fayda: agent ko baar-baar exploratory tool calls nahi karni padti data samajhne ke liye — resource seedha ek \"map\" de deta hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Resources = context ke liye read-only data, no action needed\n- Isse agent ko baar-baar \"explore karke pata karo\" wale calls nahi karne padte", "4.0.hi": "CLAUDE.md ek instruction file hai jo Claude Code ko project ke baare me batati hai — jaise ek naye employee ko diya gaya onboarding document. Ye hierarchy me kaam karti hai: **project-level** (`.claude/CLAUDE.md`, sabko milti hai, version control me) aur **user-level** (`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, sirf tumhe milti hai, personal preferences ke liye).\n\nAgar guidance sirf user-level file me hai, naye team members ko woh nahi milegi — isliye team-wide rules hamesha project-level file me honi chahiye.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Project CLAUDE.md = poori team ko milta hai (shared, version-controlled)\n- User CLAUDE.md = sirf tumhare liye (personal)\n- Team rules hamesha project-level pe rakho, warna naye members miss kar denge", "4.1.hi": "`@path` syntax ek tarah ka \"import\" hai — CLAUDE.md ke andar tum doosri files ko reference kar sakte ho bina unka content copy-paste kiye. Isse bade instruction sets ko chhote, organized files me split karna aasan ho jaata hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `@path` = modularity, ek badi file ko chhote logical parts me todne ka tareeka", "4.2.hi": "`.claude/rules/` ek directory hai jaha tum alag-alag rule files bana sakte ho jinke saath **glob patterns** attach hote hain (jaise `**/*.test.tsx`). Ye rules **automatically** apply hoti hain jab tum matching files pe kaam kar rahe ho — Claude ko manually batane ki zaroorat nahi.\n\nYe khaas useful hai jab codebase ke alag hisso me alag conventions ho (React vs API vs database) — har file-type ke liye apni rule file, apne glob pattern ke saath.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Glob patterns wali rules = path ke basis pe automatic convention application\n- Tests, alag modules, alag languages — sabke liye alag rules banayi ja sakti hain jo apne aap trigger ho jaayein", "4.3.hi": "Custom slash commands (jaise `/review`) ek shortcut hain kisi common task ke liye. **Project commands** `.claude/commands/` me jaate hain — version-controlled, sab ko milte hain repo clone karte hi. **Personal commands** `~/.claude/commands/` me jaate hain — sirf tumhare liye.\n\nAgar koi apna khud ka customized version banana chahta hai team ke command ka, use naya naam do (jaise `/my-commit`) — same naam use karne se team ka original command silently shadow ho jaata hai, aur team ke updates bhi miss ho jaate hain.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Team-wide command → `.claude/commands/` (project me)\n- Personal variant → alag naam do, warna team updates miss ho jaayenge", "4.4.hi": "Skills (`.claude/skills/`) reusable capabilities hain jo Claude Code ko diye ja sakte hain — ek `SKILL.md` file me instructions likhi hoti hain jo on-demand trigger hoti hain (keyword ya slash command se).\n\nEk khaas feature hai `context: fork` — isse skill ek **isolated subagent context** me chalti hai, taaki uska bada output ya exploration discussion main conversation ko pollute na kare aur Claude original task ka track na kho de.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Skills = on-demand invoked capabilities, hamesha load nahi hoti (CLAUDE.md ke ulat)\n- `context: fork` = bade/verbose kaam ko main session se isolate karna", "4.5.hi": "Planning mode ek \"pehle socho, phir karo\" approach hai — Claude codebase explore karta hai, dependencies samajhta hai, aur ek approach design karta hai **implementation shuru karne se pehle**.\n\nYe tab use karo jab kaam bada ho, multiple valid approaches ho, ya architectural decisions chahiye ho (jaise monolith ko microservices me todna). Direct execution simple, clear-cut tasks ke liye theek hai — lekin complex, ambiguous kaamon ke liye planning mode risk kam karta hai kyunki galat direction me expensive rework nahi hota.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Bade/ambiguous/architectural kaam → planning mode\n- Simple, clear kaam → direct execution\n- Planning mode = safe exploration + informed decision before commitment", "4.6.hi": "`/compact` command conversation history ko summarize karke chhota kar deta hai — jab context window bharne laga ho, isse purani baatein compress ho jaati hain taaki naye kaam ke liye jagah bane.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `/compact` = context window ko manually free karne ka tareeka jab woh bhar raha ho", "4.7.hi": "`/memory` command Claude Code ki memory settings (jaise CLAUDE.md) manage karne ka tareeka hai — isse tum dekh/edit kar sakte ho ki Claude ko kya-kya \"yaad\" rakhna hai across sessions.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `/memory` = persistent instructions (CLAUDE.md jaisi cheezein) manage karne ka command", "4.8.hi": "Claude Code ko CI/CD pipelines me chalane ke liye **headless/non-interactive mode** chahiye hota hai — `-p` ya `--print` flag use karke. Isse Claude prompt process karta hai, result stdout pe print karta hai, aur exit ho jaata hai — bina kisi interactive input ka wait kiye.\n\nBina isके, pipeline hang ho jaata hai kyunki Claude Code interactive input maang raha hota hai jo CI environment me kabhi nahi milega.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- CI/CD me Claude Code chalane ke liye hamesha `-p` (ya `--print`) flag use karo\n- Bina isके pipeline indefinitely hang ho jaayega", "4.9.hi": "`fork_session` session management ka ek feature hai jisse tum ek existing conversation state ko \"branch\" kar sakte ho — bilkul git branch jaisa. Isse tum ek session ke multiple variations explore kar sakte ho bina original ko affect kiye.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `fork_session` = git branch jaisa concept, ek session se multiple paths explore karna", "5.0.hi": "Few-shot prompting ka matlab hai Claude ko kuch **concrete examples** dikhaana instructions ke saath — bilkul jaise ek naye employee ko \"aisa karo\" bolne ki jagah 2-3 already-done examples dikha dena.\n\nYe tab sabse zyada kaam aata hai jab abstract instructions (\"acha output do\") consistent results nahi de rahe. Examples model ko ek concrete pattern dete hain follow karne ke liye — jo vague rules se zyada reliable hota hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Jab instructions alone inconsistent result de rahe ho, few-shot examples try karo\n- 3-6 achhe examples aksar detailed instructions se zyada effective hote hain", "5.1.hi": "Vague instructions jaise \"comments accurate hone chahiye\" Claude ko confuse karte hain — kya \"accurate\" maane? Iski jagah **explicit criteria** do: \"comment ko sirf tab flag karo jab woh claim kare kuch jo code actually nahi karta.\"\n\nExplicit criteria root cause address karti hai — ek precise definition dena ki problem kya hai, instead of ek abstract adjective.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Vague words (\"good\", \"accurate\", \"appropriate\") → explicit, checkable criteria me convert karo\n- Precise criteria false positives aur misses dono kam karti hai", "5.2.hi": "Prompt chaining ka matlab hai ek bade task ko chhote, sequential steps me todna — har step ka output agle step ka input banta hai. Jaise ek assembly line: pehle issues identify karo, phir fixes generate karo — do alag specialized prompts, ek ke baad ek.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Prompt chaining = ek complex task ko sequential, specialized steps me todna\n- Har step apna kaam achhe se karta hai kyunki uska focus narrow hai", "5.3.hi": "\"Interview\" pattern ka matlab hai Claude ko poochne dena clarifying questions taaki ambiguity resolve ho, jaise ek interviewer candidate se details poochta hai.\n\nYe useful hai jab requirements genuinely unclear ho, lekin overuse mat karo — bohot zyada questions poochna users ko frustrate kar sakta hai (abandonment badhata hai). Trade-off samajhna zaroori hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Clarifying questions genuinely ambiguous cases ke liye theek hain\n- Bohot zyada questions = user frustration; reasonable assumptions ke saath aage badhna aksar behtar hota hai", "5.4.hi": "Validation aur retry-with-feedback ka matlab hai: Claude ke output ko check karo (validate), aur agar galat hai toh use specific feedback ke saath dobara try karne do — sirf \"phir se karo\" nahi, balki \"ye specific cheez galat thi, ise fix karo\".\n\nYe semantic errors (jahan JSON syntax sahi hai lekin values galat hain) handle karne ka main tareeka hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Validation = output check karna\n- Retry-with-feedback = specific error batao, generic \"try again\" nahi\n- Ye semantic (meaning) errors fix karta hai, syntax errors nahi (unke liye schema kaafi hai)", "5.5.hi": "Self-correction ka matlab hai Claude ko apna khud ka output critique karne dena finalize karne se pehle — jaise ek writer apna draft dobara padh ke improve karta hai.\n\nLekin dhyan rahe: agar wahi Claude jisne content banaya, use khud review kare, confirmation bias ho sakta hai (\"maine sahi kiya hoga\" wali soch). Isliye kabhi-kabhi ek **independent second reviewer** (fresh eyes, bina original reasoning dekhe) zyada effective hota hai self-correction se.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Self-correction helpful hai lekin confirmation bias ka risk hai\n- Critical cases me independent review (fresh eyes) self-correction se zyada reliable hai", "6.0.hi": "Message Batches API ek \"bulk order\" jaisa hai — bohot saari requests ek saath bhejo, 50% sasta padta hai, lekin turant result nahi milta (24 ghante tak lag sakte hain). Ye synchronous (turant) API se bilkul alag hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Batch API = 50% sasta, lekin slow aur no guaranteed latency\n- Turant response chahiye ho toh synchronous use karo, sasta chahiye aur wait kar sakte ho toh batch", "6.1.hi": "Simple rule: agar user/system **wait kar raha hai turant jawab ka** (jaise PR merge block ho raha hai, ya customer chat kar raha hai), synchronous API use karo. Agar kaam **overnight ya scheduled** ho sakta hai (jaise weekly report, nightly analysis), Batch API use karo aur 50% bacha lo.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Blocking/real-time kaam → synchronous\n- Scheduled/flexible-deadline kaam → batch (savings ke liye)\n- Ek hi system me dono mix karna bilkul normal hai, alag workflows ke liye alag approach", "6.2.hi": "`custom_id` ek tag hai jo tum har request ke saath batch me bhejte ho, taaki jab results wapas aaye, tum pata laga sako kaunsa result kaunse original request ka tha — bilkul order number jaisa jo tumhe apna parcel identify karne me madad karta hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- `custom_id` = batch me har request ko uske result se match karne ka tareeka", "6.3.hi": "Batch requests me kuch individual items fail ho sakte hain jabki baaki succeed karte hain. Batch API isse gracefully handle karta hai — har request ka apna status hota hai, poora batch fail nahi hota agar ek item fail ho.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Ek item fail hone se poora batch fail nahi hota\n- Har request ka individual status check karna zaroori hai", "6.4.hi": "SLA (Service Level Agreement) planning ka matlab hai ye samajhna ki batch processing me **koi guaranteed turnaround time nahi** hai (up to 24 hours). Agar tumhare workflow ko ek specific deadline chahiye, batch API is uncertainty ko account karke plan karna padega.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Batch API = no strict SLA, sirf \"up to 24 hours\" ka estimate\n- Time-sensitive kaam ke liye batch pe blindly rely mat karo", "7.0.hi": "Fixed pipelines ka matlab hai ek predetermined sequence of steps — jaise ek factory assembly line jahan har product same steps se guzarta hai: Step A → Step B → Step C, hamesha isi order me.\n\nYe predictable, repetitive tasks ke liye achha hai jahan sequence hamesha same rehta hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Fixed pipeline = same steps, har baar, predictable order\n- Achha hai jab task ka structure hamesha same ho", "7.1.hi": "Dynamic adaptive decomposition ka matlab hai ki coordinator khud decide karta hai runtime pe ki task ko kaise todna hai — based on jo input mila hai. Fixed pipeline ke ulat, ye flexible hai: alag inputs ke liye alag decomposition ho sakta hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Dynamic decomposition = flexible, task ke actual content ke basis pe decide hota hai\n- Complex, varying tasks ke liye better hai jaha ek fixed sequence sabke liye fit nahi baithta", "7.2.hi": "Multi-pass code review ka matlab hai bade PRs (jisme kai files ho) ko ek hi pass me review karne ki jagah, multiple focused passes me review karna — pehle har file individually (local issues ke liye), phir ek separate pass sirf cross-file connections ke liye.\n\nYe attention dilution se bachaata hai — bohot saari files ek saath dekhne se detail miss ho jaati hai aur inconsistent feedback milta hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Bade multi-file reviews ko split karo: pehle per-file, phir integration/cross-file pass\n- Ek single bade pass me consistency aur depth dono kho jaate hain", "8.0.hi": "Escalation ka matlab hai jab AI agent ko kaam ko ek insaan ko sonp dena chahiye — bilkul ek junior employee ki tarah jo pata hone pe apne manager ko bulaata hai jab situation uske authority se bahar ho.\n\nMain trigger: **genuine policy gaps** (jab rules kuch cover hi nahi karte, jaise competitor price matching), na ki sirf \"complex\" lagne wale cases. Agent ko policy invent nahi karni chahiye jahan koi policy exist hi nahi karti.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Escalate karo jab genuine ambiguity/policy gap ho, na ki sirf mushkil lagne pe\n- AI ko policy \"banani\" nahi chahiye jaha koi rule define hi nahi hai", "8.1.hi": "Escalation patterns woh tareeke hain jinse AI ek human ko involve karti hai — jaise confidence-based (\"agar sure nahi hoon toh escalate karo\"), criteria-based (\"in specific situations me escalate karo\"), ya ek classifier ka use.\n\n**Explicit criteria with few-shot examples** sabse reliable pattern hai — ye clearly define karta hai kab escalate karna hai, self-rated confidence jaisa vague/unreliable approach nahi.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Explicit criteria (with examples) > self-rated confidence (jo confidently galat bhi ho sakta hai)\n- Clear decision boundaries agent ko batati hain kab \"apne aap\" solve karna hai aur kab \"human ko bulao\"", "8.2.hi": "Structured handoff protocols batate hain ki jab AI ek task human ko sonpe, use **kya-kya information** saath deni chahiye — jaise context, kya try kiya gaya tha, partial results, aur kyun escalate ho raha hai. Ek chhoti si sticky note ki jagah ek proper detailed handover document socho.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Achha handoff = context + attempted steps + partial results + reason\n- Bina context ke handoff human ko poora kaam dobara se start karwaata hai", "8.3.hi": "Confidence calibration ka matlab hai ye ensure karna ki AI ka \"confidence\" actually meaningful ho — jaise ek student jo genuinely samajh ke confident hai vs ek jo bas guess kar raha hai lekin confident lag raha hai.\n\nSelf-rated confidence (1-10 scale) unreliable hoti hai kyunki model confidently galat bhi ho sakta hai. Isliye human oversight ko explicit, testable criteria pe base karna chahiye, model ke apne confidence-claim pe nahi.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Model ka apna \"confidence score\" hamesha trustworthy nahi hota\n- Explicit criteria (based on facts, na ki self-assessment) zyada reliable calibration deti hai", "9.0.hi": "Errors do broad categories me aate hain: **transient** (temporary, retry karne se theek ho sakte hain — jaise network timeout) aur **permanent** (retry se kabhi theek nahi honge — jaise galat syntax wali query).\n\nInhe alag treat karna zaroori hai — dono ko same tarike se handle karna (jaise dono ko retry karna) time waste karta hai permanent errors pe.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Transient errors → retry logic se fix ho sakte hain\n- Permanent errors → retry bekar hai, seedha report karo\n- In dono ko distinguish karna hi sahi error handling ka pehla step hai", "9.1.hi": "Kuch common galtiyan jo error handling me hoti hain: (1) errors ko silently swallow karna aur success dikhaana (agent ko lagta hai sab theek hai jabki nahi tha), (2) generic error messages jo kuch decide karne ke liye information nahi dete, (3) har error pe poora system crash kar dena chahe error minor ho.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Errors ko chhupao mat — structured info ke saath surface karo\n- Ek generic \"failed\" message kaafi nahi hai — context chahiye\n- Har error ko system-wide crash mat banao, appropriately scope karo", "9.2.hi": "Ek structured subagent error me hona chahiye: error ka **type/category**, kya **retry** possible hai, exact **message**, konsi **query/action** attempt ki gayi thi, aur koi bhi **partial results** jo mile ho. Ye coordinator ko bataata hai ki aage kya decide karna hai — retry, alternative approach, ya escalate.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Structured error = type + retryable? + message + attempted action + partial results\n- Ye coordinator ko intelligent recovery decisions lene deta hai", "9.3.hi": "Jab kai subagents kaam kar rahe ho aur kuch fail ho jaayein jabki kuch succeed karein, final synthesis me **coverage annotations** add karna zaroori hai — ye batata hai kaunsi cheezein well-supported hain (saari sources se confirm) aur kahan gaps hain (kuch source unavailable tha).\n\nYe transparency ke saath graceful degradation hai — poora kaam waste nahi hota agar kuch part fail ho jaaye, lekin uncertainty bhi hide nahi hoti.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Partial failures ko chupaao mat — coverage gaps ko explicitly mark karo\n- Isse output honest rehta hai bina complete failure declare kiye", "10.0.hi": "Jab conversation lambi ho jaati hai aur summarize karni padti hai, kuch cheezein — jaise **numbers, dates, amounts** — summarization me precision kho deti hain (\"15% discount\" ban jaata hai \"kuch promotional pricing\"). Isका solution: in critical facts ko ek separate \"facts block\" me extract karo jo har prompt me include ho, chahe baaki history summarize ho jaaye.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Critical numbers/dates ko ek alag \"facts\" section me nikalo\n- Ye summarization ke precision-loss se bachata hai", "10.1.hi": "Tool results aksar zaroorat se zyada data return karte hain (jaise 40 fields jabki sirf 5 kaam ke hain). Inhe **trim** karna zaroori hai — sirf relevant fields rakho, baaki hata do — taaki context window bekaar me na bhare.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Tool outputs ko trim karo — sirf jo zaroori hai wahi context me jaana chahiye\n- Bade, verbose outputs context window jaldi bhar dete hain", "10.2.hi": "Position-aware input ka matlab hai ye samajhna ki Claude lambi input ke **shuruat aur end** ko sabse reliably process karta hai — beech ka hissa miss ho sakta hai (\"lost in the middle\"). Isliye important information (jaise key findings summary) ko shuruat me rakho, aur detailed content ko clear section headings ke saath organize karo taaki navigation aasan ho.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Sabse important info shuruat ya end me rakho, beech me nahi\n- Section headings model ko beech ke content navigate karne me madad karte hain", "10.3.hi": "Scratchpad files ek tarah ki \"working notes\" hain jo agent apne kaam ke dauran maintain karta hai — jaise ek programmer ke to-do comments jo unhe track karne me madad karte hain kya kar chuke hain aur kya baaki hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Scratchpad = agent ka apna \"working memory\" jo complex, multi-step tasks me progress track karta hai\n\nJab context \"degrade\" hone lagta hai (bohot bhar jaata hai ya purana ho jaata hai), critical structured facts (jaise class hierarchies, rate limits, migration history) ko compact bullet points me summarize karke rakhna chahiye — taaki important technical details na kho jaayein chahe baaki conversation summarize ho jaaye.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Technical/structural facts (jaise \"X inherits from Y\", \"rate limit 100/min\") ko explicitly preserve karo\n- Ye ek \"key findings\" cheat-sheet ki tarah kaam karta hai jab poori history summarize honi ho", "10.4.hi": "Jab ek task bohot verbose output produce karta hai (jaise \"120 files me saare error-handling patterns dhoondo\"), use ek **subagent ko delegate** kar do jo apne alag context me kaam kare aur sirf ek concise summary wapas de. Isse main conversation ka context window verbose discovery output se bharne se bach jaata hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Verbose/exploratory kaam ko subagent me isolate karo\n- Main conversation ko sirf summary milta hai, poora raw output nahi", "10.5.hi": "Structured state persistence ka matlab hai agent ki progress ko **structured format** (jaise JSON) me kahin save karna, taaki agar system crash ho ya restart ho, kaam wahi se resume ho sake jahan chhoda tha — sab kuch se scratch se dobara shuru nahi karna padta.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Structured state = crash recovery ka insurance\n- Lambe, multi-step tasks ke liye ye progress ko safe rakhta hai", "11.0.hi": "Attribution loss ka matlab hai jab information ka \"source\" (kahan se aayi) summarization ya processing ke dauran kho jaata hai. Jaise ek fact tha \"Reuters ke mutabik X hua\", lekin summarize hone ke baad sirf \"X hua\" bach jaata hai — ab pata nahi konse source ne ye kaha tha.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Har fact ke saath uska source track karte raho, especially summarization ke dauran\n- Bina attribution, verification aur trust dono impossible ho jaate hain", "11.1.hi": "Jab do sources ek hi cheez ke baare me contradictory information dein (jaise ek report 40% growth kahe, doosri 12%), best approach hai **dono values ko preserve karna with clear source attribution**, aur reconciliation (kaunsa sahi hai decide karna) ko coordinator jaise higher-level component ko sonpna — jiske paas broader context hota hai.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Conflicting data ko silently pick mat karo — dono ko attribution ke saath rakho\n- Reconciliation ka decision us component ko do jiske paas sabse zyada context ho", "11.2.hi": "Dates include karna zaroori hai kyunki bina date ke ek fact ka matlab galat nikl sakta hai — jaise \"unemployment 5% hai\" agar 2020 ka data hai vs 2024 ka, dono ka matlab bilkul alag hai. Dates context ko sahi tareeke se interpret karne me madad karti hain.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Har fact/data point ke saath uski date bhi carry karo\n- Bina date ke, purana data naye jaisa treat ho sakta hai (galat conclusion)", "11.3.hi": "\"Render by content type\" ka matlab hai different types ke content (jaise numbers, quotes, dates, categories) ko unke apne appropriate format me present karna, ek generic text blob me sab kuch mix karne ki jagah — taaki har type ki information apni original clarity ke saath samajh aaye.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Alag content types (numeric, categorical, textual) ko unke apne sahi format me render karo\n- Isse information clear aur unambiguous rehti hai", "12.0.hi": "Claude Code ke paas kuch built-in tools hain — **Read** (file padhna), **Write** (nayi file banana), **Edit** (existing file me changes karna), **Bash** (terminal commands chalana), **Grep** (text search karna files me), **Glob** (file patterns dhoondhna, jaise saari `.test.js` files).\n\nHar tool ka apna sahi use-case hai — jaise Grep text-content search ke liye, Glob filename patterns ke liye.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Read/Write/Edit = file operations\n- Bash = command execution\n- Grep = content search, Glob = filename pattern search", "12.1.hi": "Incremental investigation strategy ka matlab hai ek bade, unfamiliar codebase ko step-by-step explore karna — pehle high-level structure dekho (Glob/directory listing), phir relevant files dhoondo (Grep), phir unhe detail me padho (Read) — sab kuch ek saath try karne ki jagah.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Bade codebase ko samajhne ke liye broad se narrow ki taraf jao\n- Ek step ka result agla step decide karta hai (incremental, na ki ek saath sab kuch)", "12.2.hi": "Kabhi-kabhi Edit tool directly kaam nahi karta (jaise agar file bohot complex hai ya edit pattern match nahi ho raha), toh fallback approach hai: **Read** karke poora content dekho, phir **Write** se poori file naye content ke saath overwrite kar do.\n\n**Yaad rakhne wali baat:**\n- Edit fail ho ya complicated ho toh Read + Write ek reliable fallback hai\n- Ye poori file ko dobara likhta hai instead of ek chhota sa targeted change", "0.0.en": "Think of it like ordering food from a waiter who has zero memory — every single time, you have to repeat your entire order from scratch.\n\nThat's how the Claude API works: every request is a complete package containing the **model** (which Claude), **max_tokens** (how long the answer can be), **system** (how Claude should behave), and **messages** (the whole conversation so far). You can also give Claude tools to use (`tools`), and `tool_choice` controls whether using a tool is required or optional.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- The `messages` array must contain the **full conversation history** — Claude doesn't remember anything on its own\n- `system` is a separate field, and it applies for the whole conversation\n- `tools` and `tool_choice` decide what Claude is allowed to call", "0.1.en": "In a chat, two people talk — `user` and `assistant`. But when Claude uses a tool (like checking a database), the result is sent back inside a `user`-role message, wrapped in a special `tool_result` block — there's no separate \"tool\" role.\n\nThe `system` field is normally set once at the top level, but it can also be inserted mid-conversation as a special message — useful for adding new instructions partway through without invalidating earlier cached context. There are strict rules about where it can appear.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Tool result = a `user`-role message containing a `tool_result` block, not a separate role\n- Every API call is independent — you must resend the full history each time\n- A mid-conversation `system` message overrides earlier ones for turns that follow", "0.2.en": "`stop_reason` is like a \"reason code\" on Claude's reply — it tells you why Claude stopped generating.\n\nThe two most important values: `tool_use` (Claude wants to call a tool — you execute it and send the result back) and `end_turn` (Claude's answer is complete — show it to the user). The others are `max_tokens` (hit the limit, answer may be cut off) and `stop_sequence`.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Agentic loops are controlled entirely by this field\n- `tool_use` = run the tool and return the result; `end_turn` = done\n- Don't guess completion from Claude's text — `stop_reason` is the reliable signal", "0.3.en": "The system prompt is like a \"job description\" given to Claude at the start of a conversation — it defines who Claude is, how it should behave, and what rules to follow.\n\nIt has higher priority than user messages and stays constant throughout. But be careful: if the system prompt has wording like \"always verify the customer,\" Claude might take that too literally and overuse a verification tool even when it isn't needed.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- System prompt = the place to define role, constraints, and output format\n- Wording matters a lot — small phrases can create unintended tool-usage patterns\n- It's not part of the `messages` array; it's a separate field", "0.4.en": "The context window is Claude's \"working memory\" — how much text (in tokens) it can see at once. It includes the system prompt, the full message history, tool definitions, and tool results.\n\nThree common problems: (1) **Lost-in-the-middle** — Claude reliably remembers the start and end of long input but can miss the middle (like forgetting the middle chapter of a long book). (2) **Tool result accumulation** — if a tool returns 40 fields but only 5 matter, the other 35 waste space. (3) **Progressive summarization** — when history gets summarized, exact numbers/dates turn into vague words like \"some\" or \"roughly.\"\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Put important information near the start or end, not buried in the middle\n- Trim tool outputs — keep only the fields you need\n- Watch numbers/dates carefully during summarization, or precision gets lost", "1.0.en": "Imagine Claude as a manager who doesn't personally do the coding — it just tells the team what to do. `tool_use` works the same way: Claude doesn't run code itself; it says \"run this function with these parameters,\" your code actually executes it, and the result goes back to Claude.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Claude only generates the request; your code does the execution\n- This is the main way Claude connects to the \"real world\" (databases, APIs, files, anything)", "1.1.en": "Every tool is defined with a JSON schema — it has a name, a description, and an input format. The most important piece is the **description** — it's Claude's primary way of deciding which tool to use and when.\n\nIf the description is minimal (\"retrieves customer info\"), Claude can get confused when two tools look similar. A good description includes: what the tool does, what it returns, example input formats, edge cases, and when to use it versus a similar tool. Claude can also get confused between built-in tools (like Read, Grep) and MCP tools if the MCP tool's description is weak.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Tool descriptions are the model's **primary selection mechanism** — make them detailed\n- Never let two tools have identical or overlapping descriptions\n- Every description should cover: what it does, input format, and when to use it vs. alternatives", "1.2.en": "`tool_choice` controls how much freedom Claude has in picking a tool. Three options: `auto` (Claude decides — the default), `any` (Claude must call some tool), and a specific tool name (Claude must call exactly that tool).\n\nThis is useful when you need guaranteed structured output (use `any`), or when you need to force a specific first step (like always running `extract_metadata` first).\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- `auto` = default, flexible\n- `any` = guarantees structured output\n- Naming a specific tool = forces that exact first step", "1.3.en": "If you need structured JSON from Claude (like filling out a form), `tool_use` plus a JSON schema is the most reliable method. It guarantees the JSON will be syntactically correct (no missing brackets) and required fields will be present — but it does **not** guarantee the values are correct (Claude can still give wrong data).\n\nGood schema design rules: (1) Don't mark fields as required if the data isn't always available — otherwise Claude will invent fake values. (2) Use nullable fields for information that might be missing, so Claude can return `null` instead of hallucinating. (3) Add an \"other\" option in categories (enums) to handle unexpected cases. (4) If Claude isn't confident, an \"unclear\" option is better than a wrong category.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- JSON schema guarantees syntax, not meaning\n- Nullable fields and \"other\"/\"unclear\" options prevent hallucination\n- Only mark fields `required` if the data is truly always available", "1.4.en": "There are two kinds of mistakes: **Syntax errors** (like invalid JSON, wrong field type) and **Semantic errors** (like numbers not adding up, wrong value in the right field, hallucination).\n\nSyntax errors can be completely eliminated using `tool_use` with a JSON schema. Semantic errors are different — they need validation checks, retry-with-feedback, or self-correction.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Syntax fix = schema enforcement (100% solvable)\n- Semantic fix = validation + retry logic (a schema alone won't catch this)", "2.0.en": "Think of a chef following a recipe step by step: pick up an ingredient, check it, do the next step, until the dish is done. An agentic loop works the same way — Claude doesn't give one answer, it performs a series of actions: send a request → check the response → if it's `tool_use`, run the tool and send the result back → check again → when it's `end_turn`, stop.\n\nThis is **model-driven** — Claude itself decides which tool to run next, based on context. That's different from a fixed decision-tree where the sequence is hardcoded in advance.\n\n**Mistakes to avoid:** looking for phrases like \"Task completed\" in Claude's text to decide when to stop, using an arbitrary limit (like max 5 iterations), or assuming that any text content means the task is done.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- There's only one reliable signal: `stop_reason == \"end_turn\"`\n- This is model-driven, not a hardcoded sequence", "2.1.en": "`AgentDefinition` is an agent's \"configuration card\" — it holds the name, description, system_prompt (instructions), and `allowed_tools` (which tools it can use).\n\n`allowed_tools` is especially important — it follows the \"least privilege\" principle: give the agent only the tools it actually needs, nothing extra.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- `allowed_tools` matters for both security and focus\n- A coordinator agent's `allowed_tools` should include `\"Task\"` so it can spawn subagents", "2.2.en": "Hub-and-spoke is an architecture pattern where a **coordinator** sits in the middle and multiple **subagents** (like search, analysis, synthesis) work around it — just like a manager and their team.\n\nThe coordinator's job: break the task down, choose the right subagents, delegate work, collect results, handle errors, and give the final answer to the user.\n\n**The most important principle:** subagents have **isolated context** — they don't automatically see the coordinator's full conversation history. Any context they need has to be explicitly included in their prompt. All communication flows through the coordinator (for observability and control).\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Subagents don't remember anything on their own — everything must be explicitly passed in\n- All communication goes through the coordinator; subagents don't talk to each other directly", "2.3.en": "The `Task` tool is the mechanism a coordinator uses to spawn new subagents. A bad way to do it: just telling a subagent \"Analyze the document\" (no context at all). The right way: giving full context — the document text, prior search results, expected output format — all written into the prompt.\n\nA key power feature: a coordinator can spawn **multiple Tasks in parallel** within a single response — like handing 3 different jobs to 3 different subagents at once, and they all run simultaneously.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Giving explicit context to a subagent is mandatory\n- Parallel Task calls = multiple subagents can work at the same time from one response", "2.4.en": "Hooks are like \"checkpoint guards\" that intercept specific points in the agent's lifecycle. Two main types: **PostToolUse** (modifies/normalizes a tool's result before Claude sees it — like making date formats consistent) and **PreToolUse** (can block an action before it happens — like stopping a refund over $500).\n\nThe most important distinction: **Hooks are deterministic (100% guaranteed)**, while **prompt instructions are probabilistic (>90%, never 100%)**. Simple rule: if a failure has financial, legal, or safety consequences, use hooks — don't just rely on prompts.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Hooks = code-level guarantee (will never be skipped)\n- Prompts = suggestions (Claude usually follows them, but no guarantee)\n- Critical business rules → hooks; general preferences → prompts", "3.0.en": "MCP (Model Context Protocol) is a standard \"plug\" used to connect Claude to outside systems — like a USB port that connects any compatible device.\n\nMCP defines three things: **Tools** (functions that perform actions — CRUD, API calls, commands), **Resources** (data the agent can read for context — docs, schemas), and **Prompts** (ready-made templates for common tasks).\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Tools = actions, Resources = read-only context data, Prompts = templates\n- It's an open standard meant to work the same way everywhere", "3.1.en": "An MCP server is a process that implements the MCP protocol and provides tools/resources. When you connect to an MCP server, all its tools are discovered automatically, and if multiple servers are connected, all their tools become available at once.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Connect one server, get all its tools\n- Tool descriptions determine how the model will use them", "3.2.en": "There are two places to configure MCP servers: **Project config** (`.mcp.json`) — for the whole team, checked into version control, using environment variables for secrets (like `${GITHUB_TOKEN}`) so actual tokens are never committed. And **User config** (`~/.claude.json`) — for personal experiments, not shared via version control.\n\nFor standard integrations (Jira, GitHub, Slack), prefer existing community MCP servers; only build your own when the workflow is truly unique to your team.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Team-wide: `.mcp.json` + env variables (keeps secrets safe)\n- Personal: `~/.claude.json`\n- Try existing servers first before building your own", "3.3.en": "When an MCP tool fails, it sets `isError: true` in the response — this tells the agent the call failed.\n\nA **good error** is structured — it tells you the error type (like \"transient\"), whether it's retryable, the exact message, and any partial results. A **generic error** (just \"Operation failed\") gives the agent no information to decide anything — should it retry, change the query, or escalate? It has no way to know.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- A good error response is structured, detailed, and includes retry info\n- A generic error is useless for the agent and makes decision-making impossible", "3.4.en": "Resources are data an agent can read without taking any action — like a content catalog (list of all tasks), a database schema, or documentation.\n\nTheir benefit: the agent doesn't need repeated exploratory tool calls to understand what data exists — a resource gives it an immediate \"map.\"\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Resources = read-only context data, no action needed\n- They avoid repeated \"explore and find out\" tool calls", "4.0.en": "CLAUDE.md is an instruction file that tells Claude Code about the project — like an onboarding document for a new employee. It works in a hierarchy: **project-level** (`.claude/CLAUDE.md`, everyone gets it, checked into version control) and **user-level** (`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, just for you, for personal preferences).\n\nIf guidance only exists in the user-level file, new team members won't get it — that's why team-wide rules should always live in the project-level file.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Project CLAUDE.md = the whole team gets it (shared, version-controlled)\n- User CLAUDE.md = just for you (personal)\n- Keep team rules at the project level, or new members will miss them", "4.1.en": "The `@path` syntax is like an \"import\" — inside CLAUDE.md you can reference other files without copy-pasting their content. This makes it easy to split large instruction sets into smaller, organized files.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- `@path` = modularity, a way to break one big file into smaller logical parts", "4.2.en": "`.claude/rules/` is a directory where you can create separate rule files, each attached to a **glob pattern** (like `**/*.test.tsx`). These rules apply **automatically** whenever you're working on matching files — you don't have to tell Claude manually.\n\nThis is especially useful when different parts of a codebase have different conventions (React vs. API vs. database) — one rule file per file-type, each with its own glob pattern.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Glob-pattern rules = automatic convention application based on file path\n- You can set up rules for tests, specific modules, or languages that trigger themselves automatically", "4.3.en": "Custom slash commands (like `/review`) are shortcuts for a common task. **Project commands** go in `.claude/commands/` — version-controlled, available to everyone the moment they clone the repo. **Personal commands** go in `~/.claude/commands/` — just for you.\n\nIf someone wants to customize their own version of a team command, give it a new name (like `/my-commit`) — reusing the same name silently shadows the team's original command, and they'd also miss future updates to it.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Team-wide command → `.claude/commands/` (in the project)\n- Personal variant → use a different name, or you'll miss team updates", "4.4.en": "Skills (`.claude/skills/`) are reusable capabilities you can give Claude Code — a `SKILL.md` file contains instructions that are triggered on-demand (by keyword or slash command).\n\nA key feature is `context: fork` — it runs the skill in an **isolated subagent context**, so its large output or exploration discussion doesn't pollute the main conversation and Claude doesn't lose track of the original task.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Skills = invoked on demand, not always loaded (unlike CLAUDE.md)\n- `context: fork` = isolates large/verbose work from the main session", "4.5.en": "Planning mode is a \"think first, then act\" approach — Claude explores the codebase, understands the dependencies, and designs an approach **before** starting implementation.\n\nUse it when the task is big, has multiple valid approaches, or requires architectural decisions (like splitting a monolith into microservices). Direct execution is fine for simple, clear-cut tasks — but for complex, ambiguous work, planning mode reduces the risk of expensive rework in the wrong direction.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Big/ambiguous/architectural work → planning mode\n- Simple, clear work → direct execution\n- Planning mode = safe exploration + an informed decision before committing", "4.6.en": "The `/compact` command summarizes and shrinks the conversation history — when the context window is filling up, this compresses older parts to make room for new work.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- `/compact` = a way to manually free up context space when it's getting full", "4.7.en": "The `/memory` command is how you manage Claude Code's memory settings (like CLAUDE.md) — it lets you view and edit what Claude should \"remember\" across sessions.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- `/memory` = manages persistent instructions (things like CLAUDE.md)", "4.8.en": "Running Claude Code in a CI/CD pipeline requires **headless/non-interactive mode** — using the `-p` or `--print` flag. This makes Claude process the prompt, print the result to stdout, and exit — without waiting for any interactive input.\n\nWithout this, the pipeline hangs because Claude Code is waiting for interactive input that will never come in a CI environment.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Always use the `-p` (or `--print`) flag when running Claude Code in CI/CD\n- Without it, the pipeline will hang indefinitely", "4.9.en": "`fork_session` is a session management feature that lets you \"branch\" an existing conversation state — just like a git branch. It lets you explore multiple variations of a session without affecting the original.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- `fork_session` = like a git branch, for exploring multiple paths from one session", "5.0.en": "Few-shot prompting means showing Claude a few **concrete examples** alongside your instructions — like showing a new employee 2-3 already-completed examples instead of just saying \"do it like this.\"\n\nIt helps most when abstract instructions (\"give a good output\") aren't producing consistent results. Examples give the model a concrete pattern to follow — which is more reliable than vague rules.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- When instructions alone give inconsistent results, try few-shot examples\n- 3-6 good examples are often more effective than detailed instructions", "5.1.en": "Vague instructions like \"comments should be accurate\" confuse Claude — what does \"accurate\" even mean? Instead, give **explicit criteria**: \"only flag a comment when it claims something the code doesn't actually do.\"\n\nExplicit criteria address the root cause — giving a precise definition of the problem instead of an abstract adjective.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Convert vague words (\"good,\" \"accurate,\" \"appropriate\") into explicit, checkable criteria\n- Precise criteria reduce both false positives and missed issues", "5.2.en": "Prompt chaining means breaking a big task into smaller, sequential steps — where each step's output becomes the next step's input. Like an assembly line: first identify issues, then generate fixes — two separate, specialized prompts, one after the other.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Prompt chaining = splitting a complex task into sequential, specialized steps\n- Each step does its job well because its focus is narrow", "5.3.en": "The \"interview\" pattern means letting Claude ask clarifying questions to resolve ambiguity — like an interviewer asking a candidate for details.\n\nUseful when requirements are genuinely unclear, but don't overuse it — too many questions can frustrate users (it increases abandonment). Understanding this trade-off matters.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Clarifying questions are fine for genuinely ambiguous cases\n- Too many questions = user frustration; proceeding with reasonable assumptions is often better", "5.4.en": "Validation and retry-with-feedback means: check Claude's output (validate), and if it's wrong, let it try again with specific feedback — not just \"try again,\" but \"this specific thing was wrong, fix it.\"\n\nThis is the main way to handle semantic errors (where the JSON syntax is fine but the values are wrong).\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Validation = checking the output\n- Retry-with-feedback = point out the specific error, not a generic \"try again\"\n- This fixes semantic (meaning) errors, not syntax errors (a schema already handles those)", "5.5.en": "Self-correction means letting Claude critique its own output before finalizing it — like a writer re-reading their draft to improve it.\n\nBut be careful: if the same Claude that produced the content also reviews it, there can be confirmation bias (\"I probably got this right\"). Sometimes an **independent second reviewer** (fresh eyes, without seeing the original reasoning) is more effective than self-correction.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Self-correction is helpful but carries a confirmation-bias risk\n- For critical cases, independent review (fresh eyes) is more reliable than self-correction", "6.0.en": "The Message Batches API is like a \"bulk order\" — send lots of requests at once, get 50% off, but you don't get the result immediately (it can take up to 24 hours). It's very different from the synchronous (instant) API.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Batch API = 50% cheaper, but slow and with no guaranteed latency\n- Need an instant response? Use synchronous. Okay to wait for cheaper? Use batch.", "6.1.en": "Simple rule: if a user or system is **waiting for an immediate answer** (like a PR merge being blocked, or a customer chatting live), use the synchronous API. If the work can be **overnight or scheduled** (like a weekly report, or nightly analysis), use the Batch API and save 50%.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Blocking/real-time work → synchronous\n- Scheduled/flexible-deadline work → batch (for savings)\n- It's completely normal to mix both approaches in the same system for different workflows", "6.2.en": "`custom_id` is a tag you attach to each request in a batch, so when results come back, you can tell which result belongs to which original request — like an order number that helps you identify your own package.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- `custom_id` = the way to match each batch request to its result", "6.3.en": "Individual items in a batch request can fail while others succeed. The Batch API handles this gracefully — each request has its own status; the whole batch doesn't fail just because one item did.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- One failed item doesn't fail the whole batch\n- You need to check each request's individual status", "6.4.en": "SLA (Service Level Agreement) planning means understanding that batch processing has **no guaranteed turnaround time** (up to 24 hours). If your workflow needs a specific deadline, you have to plan around this uncertainty when using the Batch API.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Batch API = no strict SLA, just an \"up to 24 hours\" estimate\n- Don't blindly rely on batch for time-sensitive work", "7.0.en": "Fixed pipelines mean a predetermined sequence of steps — like a factory assembly line where every product goes through the same steps: Step A → Step B → Step C, always in that order.\n\nThis works well for predictable, repetitive tasks where the sequence is always the same.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Fixed pipeline = same steps, every time, predictable order\n- Good when the task's structure is always the same", "7.1.en": "Dynamic adaptive decomposition means the coordinator decides at runtime how to break down the task — based on the actual input it received. Unlike a fixed pipeline, this is flexible: different inputs can lead to different decompositions.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Dynamic decomposition = flexible, decided by the task's actual content\n- Better for complex, varying tasks where one fixed sequence doesn't fit everything", "7.2.en": "Multi-pass code review means that instead of reviewing a large PR (with many files) in a single pass, you review it in multiple focused passes — first each file individually (for local issues), then a separate pass just for cross-file connections.\n\nThis avoids attention dilution — trying to look at too many files at once causes details to get missed and produces inconsistent feedback.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Split large multi-file reviews: per-file first, then an integration/cross-file pass\n- A single big pass loses both consistency and depth", "8.0.en": "Escalation means when an AI agent should hand a task off to a human — just like a junior employee who calls their manager when a situation is outside their authority.\n\nThe main trigger: **genuine policy gaps** (where the rules simply don't cover the situation, like competitor price matching) — not just anything that \"seems complex.\" The agent shouldn't invent a policy where none exists.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Escalate when there's a genuine ambiguity/policy gap, not just because something seems hard\n- AI shouldn't \"make up\" policy where no rule is defined", "8.1.en": "Escalation patterns are the ways an AI involves a human — like confidence-based (\"escalate if not sure\"), criteria-based (\"escalate in these specific situations\"), or using a classifier.\n\n**Explicit criteria with few-shot examples** is the most reliable pattern — it clearly defines when to escalate, unlike a vague/unreliable approach like self-rated confidence.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Explicit criteria (with examples) beats self-rated confidence (which can be confidently wrong)\n- Clear decision boundaries tell the agent when to solve things itself and when to call a human", "8.2.en": "Structured handoff protocols define what information should accompany a task when AI hands it to a human — things like context, what was already tried, partial results, and why it's escalating. Think of a proper detailed handover document instead of a tiny sticky note.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- A good handoff = context + attempted steps + partial results + reason\n- A handoff without context forces the human to start the whole task over", "8.3.en": "Confidence calibration means ensuring an AI's \"confidence\" is actually meaningful — like a student who genuinely understands vs. one who is just guessing but sounds confident.\n\nSelf-rated confidence (a 1-10 scale) is unreliable because a model can be confidently wrong. Human oversight should instead be based on explicit, testable criteria, not the model's own confidence-claim.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- A model's own \"confidence score\" isn't always trustworthy\n- Explicit criteria (based on facts, not self-assessment) give more reliable calibration", "9.0.en": "Errors fall into two broad categories: **transient** (temporary, might resolve with a retry — like a network timeout) and **permanent** (retrying will never fix it — like a badly formed query).\n\nTreating them differently matters — handling both the same way (like retrying both) wastes time on permanent errors.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Transient errors → can be fixed with retry logic\n- Permanent errors → retrying is pointless, report immediately\n- Distinguishing these is the first step of proper error handling", "9.1.en": "Some common mistakes in error handling: (1) silently swallowing errors and reporting success (the agent thinks everything is fine when it isn't), (2) generic error messages that give no information to decide anything, (3) crashing the entire system on every error, even minor ones.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Don't hide errors — surface them with structured info\n- A generic \"failed\" message isn't enough — you need context\n- Don't turn every error into a system-wide crash; scope it appropriately", "9.2.en": "A structured subagent error should include: the error's **type/category**, whether it's **retryable**, the exact **message**, what **query/action** was attempted, and any **partial results** obtained. This tells the coordinator what to decide next — retry, try an alternative approach, or escalate.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Structured error = type + retryable? + message + attempted action + partial results\n- This lets the coordinator make intelligent recovery decisions", "9.3.en": "When several subagents are working and some fail while others succeed, the final synthesis needs **coverage annotations** — these indicate what's well-supported (confirmed by all sources) and where there are gaps (some source was unavailable).\n\nThis is graceful degradation with transparency — the whole work isn't wasted if part of it fails, but uncertainty isn't hidden either.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Don't hide partial failures — explicitly mark coverage gaps\n- This keeps the output honest without declaring complete failure", "10.0.en": "When a conversation gets long and needs summarizing, certain things — like **numbers, dates, amounts** — lose precision during summarization (\"a 15% discount\" becomes \"some promotional pricing was discussed\"). The fix: extract these critical facts into a separate \"facts block\" that's included in every prompt, even if the rest of the history gets summarized.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Pull out critical numbers/dates into a separate \"facts\" section\n- This prevents the precision loss that summarization causes", "10.1.en": "Tool results often return more data than needed (like 40 fields when only 5 matter). These need to be **trimmed** — keep only the relevant fields, drop the rest — so the context window doesn't fill up unnecessarily.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Trim tool outputs — only what's necessary should enter the context\n- Large, verbose outputs fill up the context window quickly", "10.2.en": "Position-aware input means understanding that Claude processes the **start and end** of a long input most reliably — the middle can get missed (\"lost in the middle\"). So put important information (like a key-findings summary) at the start, and organize detailed content with clear section headings for easier navigation.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Put the most important info at the start or end, not buried in the middle\n- Section headings help the model navigate content in the middle", "10.3.en": "Scratchpad files are like \"working notes\" an agent keeps during its work — like a programmer's to-do comments that help track what's been done and what's left.\n\nWhen context starts \"degrading\" (getting too full or stale), critical structured facts (like class hierarchies, rate limits, migration history) should be preserved as compact bullet points — so important technical details don't get lost even if the rest of the conversation gets summarized. This acts like a \"key findings\" cheat-sheet for when the full history needs summarizing.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Scratchpad = the agent's own \"working memory\" for tracking progress on complex, multi-step tasks\n- Explicitly preserve technical/structural facts (like \"X inherits from Y,\" \"rate limit 100/min\")", "10.4.en": "When a task produces very verbose output (like \"find all error-handling patterns across 120 files\"), delegate it to a **subagent** that works in its own separate context and returns only a concise summary. This keeps the main conversation's context window from filling up with verbose discovery output.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Isolate verbose/exploratory work in a subagent\n- The main conversation only gets a summary, not the full raw output", "10.5.en": "Structured state persistence means saving an agent's progress in a **structured format** (like JSON) somewhere, so if the system crashes or restarts, work can resume where it left off — no need to start from scratch.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Structured state = insurance against crashes\n- For long, multi-step tasks, this keeps progress safe", "11.0.en": "Attribution loss means when information's \"source\" (where it came from) gets lost during summarization or processing. Like a fact that was \"According to Reuters, X happened,\" but after summarizing, only \"X happened\" remains — now you don't know which source said it.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Keep tracking each fact's source, especially during summarization\n- Without attribution, both verification and trust become impossible", "11.1.en": "When two sources give contradictory information about the same thing (like one report saying 40% growth, another saying 12%), the best approach is to **preserve both values with clear source attribution**, and hand the reconciliation (deciding which is correct) to a higher-level component like the coordinator — which has broader context.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Don't silently pick one side of conflicting data — keep both with attribution\n- Give the reconciliation decision to whichever component has the most context", "11.2.en": "Including dates matters because a fact without a date can be misinterpreted — like \"unemployment is 5%\" meaning something completely different if it's 2020 data vs. 2024 data. Dates help correctly interpret context.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Carry the date along with every fact/data point\n- Without a date, old data can be mistaken for current data (leading to wrong conclusions)", "11.3.en": "\"Render by content type\" means presenting different types of content (like numbers, quotes, dates, categories) in their own appropriate format, rather than mixing everything into one generic text blob — so each type of information keeps its original clarity.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Render different content types (numeric, categorical, textual) in their proper format\n- This keeps information clear and unambiguous", "12.0.en": "Claude Code has some built-in tools — **Read** (read a file), **Write** (create a new file), **Edit** (make changes to an existing file), **Bash** (run terminal commands), **Grep** (search text content in files), **Glob** (find files by pattern, like all `.test.js` files).\n\nEach tool has its right use-case — like Grep for content search, Glob for filename pattern search.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- Read/Write/Edit = file operations\n- Bash = command execution\n- Grep = content search, Glob = filename pattern search", "12.1.en": "Incremental investigation strategy means exploring a large, unfamiliar codebase step by step — first look at the high-level structure (Glob/directory listing), then find relevant files (Grep), then read them in detail (Read) — instead of trying to do everything at once.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- To understand a large codebase, go from broad to narrow\n- Each step's result informs the next (incremental, not all-at-once)", "12.2.en": "Sometimes the Edit tool doesn't work directly (like if the file is too complex or the edit pattern doesn't match), so the fallback approach is: **Read** the whole content, then **Write** the entire file back with the new content.\n\n**Key takeaways:**\n- If Edit fails or gets complicated, Read + Write is a reliable fallback\n- This rewrites the whole file instead of making a small targeted change"};
